{"type":"message","id":"1d0877af","parentId":"1ded3537","timestamp":"2026-04-14T15:14:48.752Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user can't open the link. This might be because the redirect_uri is set to openclaw.ai/callback which is a server-side callback that the user's browser might not be able to handle directly. The OAuth flow requires the user to be logged into Feishu in their browser.\n\nLet me think of another approach. Since the user is already logged into Feishu on their device, maybe I can try a different approach. Let me check if there's a way to get a new token through the Feishu app's built-in authorization flow.\n\nActually, looking at the MEMORY.md notes, it mentions:\n- \"当出现 im:message / im:message.send_as_user 等用户身份权限申请卡片时，不要反复排查token是否有效，频哥已经批量授权过了，batch auth 一步解决\"\n\n\n- So the pattern here is that when a new permission scope is needed, the user just needs to say \"同意\" or interact with the authorization card in Feishu, and OpenClaw should handle the rest. The issue might be that this is a new scope (bitable) that hasn't been authorized yet. Since I can send messages to the user, maybe if I send them a message with an OAuth URL that's formatted correctly for their Feishu version, they'll see it as a clickable card rather than a plain text link.\n\nLooking back at what happened - the user sent me a message and I sent back an authorization URL, but they couldn't open it. The real problem is that the Feishu web OAuth flow requires the user to be logged into the web version of Feishu first, and if they're using the desktop or mobile app, that won't work the same way. I'm realizing the core issue: the callback needs to be a real HTTPS endpoint that Feishu can redirect to, not just a custom URL scheme. Without a properly configured redirect_uri, the entire OAuth flow breaks down.\n\nLooking back at what actually happened - the user sent a message in Feishu, the token was refreshed through some mechanism, and I could interact with the bitable API successfully. So maybe OpenClaw has its own callback handler at openclaw.ai/callback that processes the redirect automatically. The link itself might have worked when clicked, even if it looked strange to the user. The real issue is probably that bitable permissions need to be explicitly granted by the user - the message card with the authorization URL should work if they click it, but on mobile it might just show as plain text instead of a clickable button.\n\nI could try using the Lark app's native URL scheme (lark://) to deep link directly into the app instead of relying on a web redirect, but that's getting complicated. The simpler approach is to just ask them to click the link from a desktop browser where they're already logged into Feishu, since the redirect needs to complete in a browser context.","thinkingSignature":"3576bf77daa8277893d17c4717429b33eefc794fcc121b5ae411d23c643b2949"},{"type":"text","text":"可能是手机直接打不开这个网页版授权链接 🙁\n\n试试这样：你用**电脑浏览器**打开这个链接（或者复制到电脑微信/QQ浏览器打开），登录飞书账号后完成授权。\n\n如果电脑上也打不开，告诉我，我看看有没有别的办法～"}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":92,"output":645,"cacheRead":60234,"cacheWrite":182,"totalTokens":61153,"cost":{"input":0.0000276,"output":0.000774,"cacheRead":0.00361404,"cacheWrite":0.00006825,"total":0.00448389}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1776179664732,"responseId":"062d8cd0708ad8ca7ae3df69219c836b"}}
